        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Over 30% of American astronauts have developed structural changes to the eye that affect vision after long-duration space flight on the International Space Station (ISS). In addition, some astronauts have elevated fluid pressures in the spine and head after ISS missions. The space flight environment is complex and several factors may contribute to these observed vision alterations. However, the movement of fluid towards the head is one of the primary factors believed to initiate vision changes during space flight. This experiment determined intracranial pressure (ICP), intraocular pressure (IOP), and ocular structures-function using state-of-the-art noninvasive techniques during conditions known to simulate microgravity induced head-ward fluid shifts in healthy astronaut-aged individuals. Investigators hypothesized that a head-ward fluid shift during microgravity exposure causes a change in translaminar pressure (ICP-IOP) at the optic nerve head, compromising ocular structures-function. Investigators aimed to test proven head-ward fluid shift countermeasures (e.g., lower body negative pressure (LBNP) and Braslet) to mitigate elevations of ICP and IOP. The same device used for pre-, in- and post-flight ICP measures of astronauts was utilized. These studies employed a whole body tilt protocol known to induce head-ward fluid shifts and raise ICP, enabling direct comparisons of IOP, ICP and ocular structures-function. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Twenty-five healthy volunteers participated in this study. Subjects were positioned supine (5 minutes), sitting (5 minutes), 15 whole body head down tilt (HDT) (5 minutes) and 10 minutes of HDT with LBNP (25 mmHg). The order of HDT and HDT+LBNP tests were balanced. Right and left IOP, transcranial ultrasound pulse amplitude, arm blood pressure, and heart rate were measured during the last minute of each testing condition.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
IOP significantly decreased from supine to sitting posture by 3.2 ± 1.4, and increased by 0.9 ± 1.3 mmHg from supine to the HDT position. LBNP during HDT significantly lowered IOP to supine levels. In addition, LBNP significantly reduced transcranial ultrasound pulse amplitudes (noninvasive ICP) by 38% as compared to the HDT condition. 
<p>
Noninvasive Cerebral and Cochlear Fluid Pressure (CFP) measures of ICP significantly increased from the sitting to the supine posture. Moreover, ICP further increased significantly when the subjects were positioned in the HDT position, as compared to supine. However, when LBNP (25 mmHg and 50 mmHg) was activated during HDT, ICP decreased significantly, as compared to HDT. Cross sectional area measures of the internal jugular vein parallel these noninvasive ICP findings. Therefore, LBNP may reduce cephalad venous congestion during simulated microgravity and reduce ICP. These data provide further evidence that LBNP may be an effective countermeasure to mitigate cephalad fluid shifts.
<p>
        </div>

